From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] The navigation bar gets a button that hides it, and a long press that brings it back

The bar can now be locked hidden (patch 0076), but the reader has no way to ask for
it. This patch gives the bar one more key: a chevron pointing down onto a line, at
the right-hand end of the bar, next to Recents. A tap hides the bar and locks it; a
long press unlocks it and drops `policy_control`, so the bar stays up until the next
boot decides again.

The nav bar's layout is a string from SystemUI's own resources --
`config_navBarLayout` in `packages/SystemUI/res/values/config.xml` -- and its keys
are inflated from it by `NavigationBarInflaterView`, so this is a new token
(`navlock`) and a new layout for it, not a change to framework-res: no resource ID
that the ART boot image depends on moves. The button is a plain `ImageView` rather
than a `KeyButtonView`, because it sends no key code.

The button does not touch Settings itself: it sends the broadcast introduced by
patch 0076, and the policy side writes the setting and applies it, so there is one
place that owns the state.

Not run on a device yet when this patch was written; the verification list is in
`readom-port/reports/t09-nav-impl.md`.

diff --git a/packages/SystemUI/res/values/config.xml b/packages/SystemUI/res/values/config.xml
--- a/packages/SystemUI/res/values/config.xml
+++ b/packages/SystemUI/res/values/config.xml
@@ -339,7 +339,9 @@
     <bool name="config_quickSettingsOnFirstPull">false</bool>
 
     <!-- Nav bar button default ordering/layout -->
-    <string name="config_navBarLayout" translatable="false">left[.5W],back[1WC];home;recent[1WC],right[.5W]</string>
+    <!-- Readom: navlock is the bar's own hide button (readom_nav_key.xml). A long press
+         on it brings the bar back. -->
+    <string name="config_navBarLayout" translatable="false">left[.5W],back[1WC];home;recent[1WC],navlock[1WC],right[.5W]</string>
 
     <bool name="quick_settings_show_full_alarm">false</bool>
 
